# System tools index

<!-- Copy to ~/.agents/system-tools-index.md and fill in. This file is
untracked on purpose: it names the tools installed on this machine, how each
got here, and when to reach for it. Agents both use and maintain it; a new
install or a trial-verified caveat earns an entry the day it lands. -->

The curated system-wide tool layer: what each tool is for and how it got
here. Scope is tools you invoke; libraries that link into builds live in each
project's manifest. Record the deployment convention (package manager,
versioned dir plus symlink, built from source) so "what's deployed from
where" has one answer.

## Owned diagnostic CLIs

Tools built in-house, with source location and the question each answers.

- <tool: what it answers, source path>

## Toolchains

Compilers, runtimes, and language servers, with versions, pins, and pairing
constraints (a language server that must match its compiler's version, for
example).

- <tool: version, deploy method, pairing notes>

## Search, files, structural

The everyday text, file, and AST tools, each with a reach-for-it-when note
and any trial-verified caveats or known bugs.

- <tool: reach for it when, caveats>

## Git

- <tool: reach for it when, caveats>

## Agent harnesses and agent-facing

Coding harnesses, MCP servers, browser automation, memory plumbing, and web
tool packages, with configuration locations and verified working state.

- <tool: what, config location, state>

## Deliberately not installed (with the reason)

The negative space keeps future sessions from re-litigating: name the tool,
the reason it was skipped, and the condition that would change the verdict.

- <tool: reason, revisit when>

Provenance: record where each verdict came from (trial ledger, research
record) so a caveat can be re-checked instead of re-trusted. New third-party
installs go through the vet-dependency checklist at the commit that
introduces them.
